{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-semantics","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan's safety argument hinges on specific Bash set-e and function-return semantics that are easy to misstate and hard to verify from prose alone.","prompt_body":"Verify the plan's claim that wrapping `reuse_slot_result` in `if … then … fi` suppresses `set -e` abort on non-zero return under all Bash versions targeted by the repo, including Bash 3.2 as required by BASH_AUTHORING.md. Check whether any code proposed inside `reuse_slot_result` after the `|| return 1` guard — particularly the `rm -f \"$target\"` call — can itself exit non-zero and trigger a `set -e` abort before the guard has a chance to fire, given the sequencing of commands in the function body. Confirm that the placement of `|| return 1` immediately after `cp -p` covers all cp failure modes enumerated in the plan (missing source, permission error, ENOSPC mid-copy) without leaving intermediate function-local variables or side effects in an inconsistent state visible to the caller. Verify the proposed changes introduce no Bash 4+ constructs anywhere in the modified function or caller block. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ledger-state","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan deliberately leaves stale ledger rows unpruned and relies on per-slot reuse-failure fallthrough for correctness; this reviewer checks whether that decision is safe across repeated invocations and whether the test scenario's ledger path is computed correctly.","prompt_body":"Examine whether intentionally leaving stale ledger rows in place (Round 1 decision 6, cited in the plan) creates a hidden cost hazard when multiple grouped slots in the same dispatcher run all hit the same stale row and each independently relaunches the fallback tool, and whether the existing `LARCH_FALLBACK_CLAUDE_WARN_THRESHOLD` signal is sufficient to surface this condition or whether it fires too late. Check whether the relaunch path itself writes a new ledger row, and if so, whether that row is appended in a way that could cause a future run to find two rows — one stale, one valid — and whether `find_group_ok_for_tool` selects the correct one. Verify that the test scenario's pre-seeded `waterfall-group-results.tsv` path is injected at the exact filesystem location `dispatch-with-waterfall.sh` computes for the group ledger, and that the stale-row hit is deterministic rather than dependent on file-system ordering or timing in the stub environment. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
